------------------
---- MONITORS ----
------------------

-- See https://wiki.hypr.land/Configuring/Basics/Monitors/
--
-- The displays are not declared here, because this file is shared: Syncthing
-- replicates it to every machine, so a monitor block written for one of them is
-- wrong on all the others. It used to hold HDMI-A-1 and DP-1 at scale 1.67 --
-- the desktop's two screens -- which the laptop then had to override with a
-- generated monitors.lua just to drive its one panel.
--
-- hosts/<hostname>.lua declares this machine's outputs instead, and returns a
-- table naming which is primary and which is secondary. The workspace rules
-- further down read those names, so the *policy* (1-5 here, 6-10 there) stays
-- in this file and shared, while the *hardware* stays per host.
--
-- The host is chosen at build time: home/hyprland.nix substitutes the
-- machine's name, taken from the flake's mkHome argument, into the path
-- below. The old runtime hostname lookup, its domain stripping, its pcall
-- and its fail-loud banner are gone.
--
-- An unknown host is NOT an evaluation error, and an earlier version of this
-- comment said it was. home/hyprland.nix only substitutes the name; nothing
-- checks the file exists, so the pcall below is what actually happens. The
-- loud failure lives in flake.nix's host-config-files check instead, which
-- fails the build when a host in hostConfigs has no file here.
local loaded, hostCfg = pcall(dofile, "@hyprSource@/hosts/@host@.lua")
if not loaded or type(hostCfg) ~= "table" then
    -- No file for this machine: no monitor declarations, which leaves Hyprland
    -- to light up whatever it finds at its preferred mode, and no primary,
    -- which makes the workspace rules below no-ops. That is a working desktop
    -- on an unknown machine, not a broken one -- copy a hosts/ file to this
    -- machine's name to pin it down.
    hostCfg = {}
end

-- Whatever the Quickshell monitor manager last applied, which it rewrites in
-- full on every successful apply. It loads after the two defaults above, so a
-- saved layout wins over them -- and until something has been applied the file
-- does not exist and the pcall makes this a no-op, leaving the defaults in
-- charge.
--
-- The panel greps this very line to decide whether to offer persistence at all;
-- without it, it applies to the running session and says so, and the file it
-- writes is never read back. Same mechanism workspace-layouts.lua uses below.
-- State, not source: quickshell writes this. pcall stays because the file does
-- not exist until the monitor panel has been used once, and dofile raises.
pcall(dofile, "@hyprState@/monitors.lua")


