-- Set programs that you use
--
-- foot rather than kitty, and the reason is what it leaves out. foot is
-- Wayland-only with no X11 path, and it has no tabs, no splits and no scripting
-- layer -- Hyprland and tmux already do all three on this desktop, so carrying
-- kitty's copy of them buys nothing. It also draws sixel, which is what put lf
-- in a foot window below, so SUPER+Q and SUPER+E now agree on the terminal.
--
-- What it costs: foot has neither remote control nor a config reload, so a
-- theme switch does not reach a foot window that is already open. The switcher
-- writes foot/theme-colors.ini and the next window reads it -- see the note in
-- theme-switcher/Theme.qml. kitty followed live over the socket in kitty.conf,
-- and it is still installed and still recoloured that way; open one when you
-- want to watch a theme land without opening a window for it.
local terminal    = "foot"

-- The file manager is lf, and the reason is the dependency set behind the
-- alternatives. dolphin was here, and it pulls 601 packages on Debian 13 -- the
-- whole of KDE Frameworks -- to open a folder. pcmanfm-qt takes 219 and needs
-- QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME set before its icons follow the theme switcher. lf takes
-- one: a single Go binary with no toolkit at all, wearing whatever palette the
-- terminal around it has.
--
-- foot is named here rather than inherited from `terminal` above, because this
-- line needs it for a reason of its own and would keep needing it if SUPER+Q
-- ever moved back. lf can put an image in its preview pane, and sixel is the
-- only graphics format it passes through. foot 1.21.0 draws sixel; kitty 0.41.1
-- does not, measured with a hand-built one and with chafa. In kitty that pane
-- falls back to coloured blocks, and the way round it -- writing kitty's own
-- protocol past lf to /dev/tty -- corrupts the panes on any file large enough to
-- take chafa a moment to decode. lf/preview carries the timings.
--
-- It costs what `terminal` above costs: an lf window open across a theme change
-- keeps the old palette until it is restarted.
--
-- `--app-id lf` is what makes the window addressable. Without it this is another
-- foot window, indistinguishable from a shell in the SUPER+TAB switcher and
-- unreachable by any windowrule that wants to treat it differently.
local fileManager = "foot --app-id lf lf"

-- Both of these used to be rofi (`-show drun` and `-show window`). The launcher
-- and the window switcher are quickshell surfaces now, which means they follow
-- the theme switcher like everything else on screen does -- rofi was the last
-- thing that stayed Tokyo Night no matter what the rest of the desktop did.
local menu         = "qs ipc call launcher toggle"
local switchWindow = "qs ipc call switcher toggle"

