---- AUTOSTART ----
-------------------

-- See https://wiki.hypr.land/Configuring/Basics/Autostart/

-- Autostart necessary processes (like notifications daemons, status bars, etc.)
-- Or execute your favorite apps at launch like this:
--
-- hl.on("hyprland.start", function () 
--   hl.exec_cmd(terminal)
--   hl.exec_cmd("nm-applet")
--   hl.exec_cmd("waybar & hyprpaper & firefox")
-- end)
hl.on("hyprland.start", function ()
  -- uwsm owns this session. It started the compositor from
  -- /usr/share/wayland-sessions/hyprland-uwsm.desktop, and
  -- wayland-wm@hyprland.desktop.service is Type=notify -- so graphical-session.target
  -- is NOT reached until the line below notifies systemd. quickshell.service
  -- and bt-agent.service are WantedBy that target and enabled, so they start
  -- here, after the environment is in place, with no race left to lose. That
  -- race is why this file used to start them by hand and why their units used
  -- to forbid `systemctl --user enable`.
  --
  -- What this replaces: a stop of graphical-session.target, a
  -- `dbus-update-activation-environment --systemd --all` behind an `env -u`
  -- blocklist, and an explicit start of a hand-written hyprland-session.target.
  -- uwsm does all three, and its source agrees with every conclusion that code
  -- was written from: prepare-env.sh exports XDG_SESSION_TYPE=wayland
  -- unconditionally -- the variable whose absence sent every Electron flatpak to
  -- X11 -- and Varnames.never_export holds NOTIFY_SOCKET, XDG_SEAT,
  -- XDG_SESSION_ID and XDG_VTNR, the same blocklist, plus XDG_SEAT_PATH and
  -- XDG_SESSION_PATH. It exports the whole environment difference rather than an
  -- allowlist, which is the trap this file fell into once. Read in the installed
  -- source on both machines: uwsm 0.26.4 here, 0.26.1 on epiphany.
  --
  -- The teardown is gone too. wayland-wm@.service declares OnSuccess= AND
  -- OnFailure=wayland-session-shutdown.target with
  -- JobMode=replace-irreversibly, so a compositor that exits and one that
  -- crashes both take the session down with them. The hyprland.shutdown handler
  -- that used to do this could not cover the crash -- which is exactly why the
  -- deleted code had to stop the target on start as well as on shutdown.
  --
  -- finalize exports WAYLAND_DISPLAY and DISPLAY, plus everything named in
  -- UWSM_FINALIZE_VARNAMES -- which /usr/share/uwsm/plugins/hyprland.sh fills
  -- with HYPRLAND_INSTANCE_SIGNATURE, HYPRLAND_CMD, HYPRCURSOR_THEME,
  -- HYPRCURSOR_SIZE, XCURSOR_SIZE and XCURSOR_THEME. That covers the cursor
  -- variables set with hl.env further down this file. It does NOT cover PATH,
  -- which this repo does not hand to uwsm's env plugin at all: PATH is set by
  -- the wrapper script itself, in home/session.nix's compositorPath (unlike
  -- calango-desktop, calango-nix has no tracked uwsm/env).
  --
  -- The `||` is the whole alarm. Started outside uwsm -- the plain "Hyprland"
  -- session entry, or a bare TTY -- finalize exits non-zero and nothing else in
  -- this desktop comes up: no shell, no portal, no idle lock. Inside uwsm a
  -- failure here is louder still: wayland-wm@.service has TimeoutStartSec=30,
  -- so systemd kills the compositor and shuts the session down rather than
  -- leaving it half-started.
  --
  -- notify's second argument is a display time in milliseconds, not a flag: 0
  -- means zero milliseconds and the banner is never seen. There is no "until
  -- dismissed" value, so it gets a minute. Icon 3 is Error, and colour 0 means
  -- the default colour for that icon.
  hl.exec_cmd("uwsm finalize || hyprctl notify 3 60000 0 "
              .. "'not a uwsm session: log out, pick Hyprland (uwsm-managed)'")

  -- hypridle is NOT started here, and the line that used to do it was starting a
  -- second copy of a daemon systemd was already running.
  --
  -- The hypridle package ships /usr/lib/systemd/user/hypridle.service and enables
  -- it, so it is WantedBy=graphical-session.target like quickshell.service and
  -- bt-agent.service above -- the same target this comment block spends forty
  -- lines explaining that uwsm reaches with the environment already in place. On
  -- suffer the enablement is a symlink at
  -- /etc/systemd/user/graphical-session.target.wants/hypridle.service, put there
  -- by the package rather than by this repo.
  --
  -- Both really did run. Caught 2026-08-11 with two live processes: pid 3131,
  -- child of this exec_cmd at 19:15:08, and pid 3192, started by the user manager
  -- one second later. It hid for months because the duplication was harmless --
  -- two `loginctl lock-session` calls at five minutes are idempotent, two `dpms
  -- off` at five and a half are the same picture, and `lock_cmd = pidof hyprlock
  -- || /usr/bin/hyprlock --config ...` already refused to start a second
  -- locker. Adding a listener that suspends the machine is what made it worth
  -- finding.
  --
  -- If a host turns up where the package does not enable it, the fix is
  -- `systemctl --user enable --now hypridle.service` on that host, not this line
  -- coming back: a hand-started daemon has no ordering against
  -- graphical-session.target, no Restart=on-failure, and no way to be restarted
  -- other than pkill.

  -- Clipboard history, read back by the quickshell picker on SUPER+SHIFT+V.
  -- cliphist only records what is handed to it, so without these two watchers
  -- running there is no history at all.
  --
  -- The text watcher skips anything advertising x-kde-passwordManagerHint, which
  -- is the mime type password managers set on a copied secret. Without the guard
  -- every password you copy lands in a plaintext db under ~/.cache and stays
  -- there for the next 750 entries. It is not airtight -- a manager that does not
  -- set the hint is still recorded -- so treat the history as readable, not safe.
  -- wl-clip-persist was tried here and removed. It is the wlroots stand-in for
  -- Plasma's Klipper: it grabs ownership of the selection so clipboard contents
  -- survive the program that copied them exiting. Two findings against it:
  --
  --   1. It did not fix Bitwarden, which was why it was installed. A mime-type
  --      probe sampling the selection every 0.3s recorded ZERO selection events
  --      while copying from Bitwarden under --ozone-platform=wayland. The app
  --      never creates an offer there at all, so there is nothing to persist.
  --   2. It appears to break the XWayland clipboard. Bitwarden on x11 worked
  --      before it was installed, failed while it was running, and worked again
  --      once it was stopped. Hyprland bridges the X11 selection into the
  --      Wayland one, and a second owner contending for that selection is a
  --      plausible way for an XWayland copy to vanish. Single A/B, not proof.
  --
  -- Binary is still at ~/.cargo/bin/wl-clip-persist if this is worth revisiting.

  -- Guarded with pgrep because a second watcher on the same mime type means every
  -- copy is stored twice, and the history fills with pairs. Kept as a belt-and-
  -- braces measure: hyprland.start does NOT fire on `hyprctl reload` -- tested
  -- with a marker command in this handler, which stays unwritten across a reload
  -- -- so the only known trigger is compositor startup, where nothing is running
  -- to duplicate.
  --
  -- This used to cite the unguarded hypridle above as a second proof, on the
  -- grounds that it never produced a duplicate either. It did produce one, every
  -- login, just not from a reload; see the block where that line used to be. The
  -- reload finding stands on the marker test alone.
  hl.exec_cmd("pgrep -f 'wl-paste --type text --watch' >/dev/null || "
              .. "wl-paste --type text --watch sh -c '"
              .. "wl-paste --list-types | grep -qi x-kde-passwordManagerHint || cliphist store'")
  hl.exec_cmd("pgrep -f 'wl-paste --type image --watch' >/dev/null || "
              .. "wl-paste --type image --watch cliphist store")
end)


